WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.180
Parser-safe C starts by treating protocol input as untrusted until framing, field validation, numeric checks, and state checks succeed.

2
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:15.720
A message becomes meaningful only after the parser proves that it satisfies the protocol contract.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.120
Keep raw bytes, decoded fields, validated messages, and trusted application objects clearly separated.

4
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:30.660
This separation prevents partially checked input from leaking into business logic and gives reviewers a way to inspect each transition.

5
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:37.740
Parse incrementally with explicit buffer capacity, used length, consumed length, and remaining input state.

6
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:46.000
Handle partial reads, incomplete messages, overlong messages, rejected input, and disconnects as normal conditions.

7
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.980
Binary payloads should remain bytes unless text rules are explicit.

8
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:58.860
Validate lengths, counts, offsets, flags, versions, and command types before use.

9
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.920
Check arithmetic before allocation, indexing, copying, or pointer movement.

10
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:09.540
A message that is structurally correct still needs state and authorization checks before dispatch.

11
00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:15.980
Finally, bound resource use and fail safely when input violates the protocol contract.

12
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:23.760
Use tests, fuzzing, sanitizers, static analysis, logging discipline, and review as layered support.

13
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:31.860
Good parsers are not just clever decoders; they are reliable boundaries between external bytes and trusted application behavior.
